1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,677
Cognitive dissonance refers to the mental discomfort experienced when a person holds two

3
00:00:10,677 --> 00:00:15,230
or more contradictory beliefs, values or attitudes simultaneously.

4
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:21,707
This theory, developed by psychologist Leon Festinger in the 1950s, suggests that people are

5
00:00:21,707 --> 00:00:27,000
motivated to reduce this discomfort by changing their attitudes, beliefs or behaviors.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,200
For example, imagine someone who values health but smokes cigarettes.

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:38,270
The conflict between their behavior (smoking) and their belief (valuing health) creates cognitive dissonance.

8
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:42,624
To reduce this discomfort, they might decide to quit smoking

9
00:00:42,624 --> 00:00:47,280
or rationalize their behavior by downplaying the health risks associated with smoking.

10
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:52,080
Cognitive dissonance can also play a significant role in decision making.

11
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:57,165
When faced with a difficult choice, people often experience dissonance after making a decision,

12
00:00:57,165 --> 00:01:00,200
particularly if the chosen option has downsides.

13
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:05,181
To alleviate this discomfort, they may seek out information that supports their choice

14
00:01:05,181 --> 00:01:07,290
and ignore information that contradicts it.

15
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:14,240
Understanding cognitive dissonance is crucial for psychologists because it explains various aspects of human behavior

16
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:20,210
including why people sometimes act in ways that contradict their stated beliefs and values.

17
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:25,040
Next, we will discuss some experiments conducted to study cognitive dissonance

18
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,010
and their implications for psychological research.
